{
  "id": "Compound::DB01047 - Gene::2908",
  "verbalization": "Fluocinonide, Halobetasol Propionate, and Desonide are all corticosteroids that bind to the Nr3c1 gene, which encodes the glucocorticoid receptor. This binding is supported by multiple paths in the dataset, all of which indicate a direct interaction between these compounds and the receptor. The shared pathway involves the binding of these corticosteroids to Serpina6 or directly to Nr3c1, with the glucocorticoid receptor being a common target across all three compounds. The lowest common ancestors of these entities are consistently identified as Organic Molecular Entities and Therapeutic Glucocorticoids, reinforcing their classification as corticosteroids. The evidence suggests that Fluocinonide and its analogs exert their biological effects through a common mechanism involving the glucocorticoid receptor, with Nr3c1 being a central mediator in this process. The integration of these paths highlights the role of Nr3c1 as a key target for corticosteroid action, underscoring the importance of glucocorticoid receptor signaling in the pharmacological effects of these compounds.",
